WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.159
The Sun does not cross the sky. The
Earth spins, and carries you round.

2
00:00:10.379 --> 00:00:16.137
Round the Sun go eight planets, a
belt of rock, and comets made of ice.

3
00:00:16.637 --> 00:00:20.910
The Earth spins once every
twenty-four hours. One spin, one day.

4
00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:27.585
It spins on its axis: an imaginary
line through the middle, pole to pole.

5
00:00:27.805 --> 00:00:33.331
When your side faces the Sun it is
day. At midday the Sun looks high.

6
00:00:33.551 --> 00:00:37.266
Turn a quarter further and
the Sun looks low. Sunset.

7
00:00:37.486 --> 00:00:44.359
Another quarter and your side faces away.
Night. The Sun did not move. You did.

8
00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:48.202
The spin swings your shadow
round, and changes its length.

9
00:00:48.702 --> 00:00:52.742
The Sun is a star: a huge
ball of hot, glowing gas.

10
00:00:52.962 --> 00:00:57.792
It sits at the centre of the Solar
System, and eight planets go round it.

11
00:00:58.012 --> 00:01:01.773
Nearest is Mercury. Then
Venus, then Earth, then Mars.

12
00:01:01.993 --> 00:01:06.312
Those four are small and rocky.
Earth is third, not first.

13
00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:11.223
Further out come the giants:
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.

14
00:01:11.443 --> 00:01:17.387
Jupiter is the biggest. Saturn has the
great rings, and Neptune is farthest out.

15
00:01:17.887 --> 00:01:22.067
Between Mars and Jupiter is a
belt of rocks called asteroids.

16
00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:27.070
An asteroid can be as small as a house,
or hundreds of kilometres across.

17
00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:32.305
From the cold edge of the Solar System
come comets: balls of ice and dust.

18
00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:36.891
Near the Sun its ice turns to gas,
streaming out as a glowing tail.

19
00:01:37.111 --> 00:01:42.451
A comet is not a shooting star. It
goes round the Sun, far out in space.

20
00:01:42.671 --> 00:01:48.058
A star, planets, asteroids and
comets: that is a planetary system.

21
00:01:48.558 --> 00:01:52.552
Pictures squeeze the planets
together so they fit on the page.

22
00:01:52.772 --> 00:01:57.927
A scale model shrinks everything by
the same amount: sizes and distances.

23
00:01:58.147 --> 00:02:01.816
Let the Sun be a football,
about twenty centimetres across.

24
00:02:02.036 --> 00:02:05.519
Then the Earth is a peppercorn,
twenty-five metres away.

25
00:02:05.739 --> 00:02:08.850
Jupiter is a grape, a hundred
and thirty metres off.

26
00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:14.457
Neptune is a small pea, seven hundred and
fifty metres away: most of a kilometre.

27
00:02:14.957 --> 00:02:18.394
For two thousand years people
put the Earth at the centre.

28
00:02:18.614 --> 00:02:23.583
It matched what you see. The Sun
rises, crosses the sky and sets.

29
00:02:23.803 --> 00:02:28.540
Then Copernicus did the sums. They
fitted better with the Sun at the centre.

30
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.893
Galileo's telescope saw four
moons going round Jupiter.

31
00:02:33.113 --> 00:02:37.432
He saw Venus change phase too. Not
everything went round the Earth.

32
00:02:37.652 --> 00:02:42.435
The evidence won. Knowledge changes
when enquiry brings evidence.

33
00:02:42.935 --> 00:02:48.183
The Earth spins, so the Sun only seems
to move, and day turns into night.

34
00:02:48.403 --> 00:02:52.582
Eight planets go round the Sun,
with asteroids and comets too.

35
00:02:52.802 --> 00:02:58.886
A scale model keeps sizes and distances
true. Evidence put the Sun at the centre.
